  "query_preview": "..\n\nPortions of Southeastern Arizona is currently under scattered\nmid to high level clouds, mainly Tucson south and eastward.\nMoisture from Hurricane Marie has moved into the region, with\nthe main plume of moisture to the north of our neck of the\nwoods, however PWs have increased especially in Western Pima\nCounty at 1.6 inches. The rest of Southeastern Arizona still\nhas plenty of moisture elsewhere with PWs between 1.3 to 1.5\ninches. The trough to the northwest that has been keeping the SW\nflow aloft over the pas days will slowly begin to move eastward\nand shift the flow to be Southerly as the high pressure begins\nto slowly drift back west today. Expect a few more thunderstorms\nthan yesterday, but not by much. Best chances for storms remain\nalong the International Border and along the AZ/NM border where\nthe best instability is. With any storm, locally heavy rainfall\nand isolated flash flooding. Current CAMs are also showing \noutflows from Sonora, Mexico coming in from the south in the \nevening, so overnight activity can't be ruled out tomorrow \nnight.\n\nCoverage in storms looks to increase gradually this work week,\nwith the best instability and forcing in the later half of the\nweek. As always with the monsoon, it's hard to see which day\nwill be the most active out of the bunch, but details will\nbecome clearer as we get closer. With the available moisture and\nweak flow aloft, the primary threat continues to be the flash\nflooding potential from efficient heavy rainers, with strong\noutflow winds as a secondary threat.\n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nWednesday, then dropping to below normal levels late next week.",
